            C) It decreases motor speed by increasing switching losses.
            D) It has no effect on speed — only the H-bridge direction inputs matter.
        -->

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-motors-day11">
        <title>Day 11 In-Class: Motors, the H-Bridge, and PWM</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we turn the reading into hardware.  We will follow the actuator
                signal chain that carries a number out of the STM32C031C6 and turns it
                into motion, work out how an H-bridge reverses a motor and how the TB6612
                driver exposes that through a few logic pins, and then wire a motor and
                drive it — first by hand, to set its direction, and then under a program
                that ramps its speed with PWM.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-actuator-chain">
            <title>Part 1: The Actuator Signal Chain</title>
            <p>
                On Day 7 we drew the <term>sensor signal chain</term>: a physical
                quantity enters from the world, a sensor turns it into a voltage, an
                analog front end conditions it, the ADC turns it into an integer, and the
                MCU computes with that integer.  Driving an actuator runs the same chain
                in reverse.  A number leaves the MCU, a converter turns it into a voltage,
                a driver raises that to the larger voltage and current the actuator needs,
                and the actuator turns it into a physical quantity — here, the rotation of
                a motor.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-actuator-chain">
                <caption>The actuator signal chain, the mirror of the sensor chain from
                Day 7.  The MCU produces an integer command; a converter turns it into a
                voltage; a driver raises that to the voltage and current the actuator
                needs; and the actuator turns it into motion.  Because the STM32C031C6 has
                no true digital-to-analog converter, a PWM signal stands in for the
                converter — which is why this chapter builds both the driver and the
                PWM.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/actuator-signal-chain.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Two stages of that chain are new, and they are what this chapter is about:
                the <term>driver</term>, which we meet as the TB6612 motor driver IC, and
                the converter, which on our chip is a PWM signal rather than a true
                <term>DAC</term> (digital-to-analog converter, the counterpart of the ADC
                from Day 7).  The reading gave the two facts we will lean on all hour: a
                motor's direction is set by the polarity of its voltage, and its speed by
                the average value of that voltage.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-recap">
                <title>Recall: voltage and the motor</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Before we build anything, recall from the reading and the video how
                    a DC motor responds to what we do at its terminals.  Answer both from
                    your physical intuition.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-recap-speed">
                    <statement><p>You raise the voltage across a motor's terminals while it
                    is running freely.  Does the steady speed go up, go down, or stay the
                    same?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-recap-direction">
                    <statement><p>Instead you swap the two terminals, so the same voltage
                    is applied with the opposite polarity.  What changes — the speed, the
                    direction, or both?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor>
                <p>Raising the voltage raises the steady speed, since the speed is roughly
                proportional to the terminal voltage.  Swapping the terminals reverses the
                polarity, which reverses the current and the force, so the direction
                reverses while the speed — set by the size of the voltage, which has not
                changed — stays the same.  These are the two knobs we spend the rest of the
                hour building hardware for.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-hbridge">
            <title>Part 2: The H-Bridge Reverses the Motor</title>
            <p>
                Reversing a motor means reversing the polarity of the voltage across it,
                and in an embedded system there is no hand to swap the two wires.  The
                <term>H-bridge</term> does it electronically.  As we saw in the reading
                (<xref ref="fig-hbridge-concept"/>), it is four switches around the motor:
                two upper switches to the motor supply and two lower switches to ground.
                Each switch is one of the transistors we met on Day 6 — the upper two are
                P-channel switches, on when their gate is LOW, and the lower two are
                N-channel switches, on when their gate is HIGH.
            </p>
            <p>
                Closing one switch on the upper rail and the opposite switch on the lower
                rail connects one motor terminal to the supply and the other to ground, so
                current runs through the motor.  Closing the other diagonal pair connects
                the terminals the other way round, and the current — and the rotation —
                reverses.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-diagonal">
                <title>Which switches turn the motor?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Look at the H-bridge in <xref ref="fig-hbridge-concept"/>, whose
                    output terminals we will call OUT1 (left) and OUT2 (right).  We want
                    current to flow out of OUT1, through the motor, and back into OUT2.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-diagonal-cw">
                    <statement><p>Which two of the four switches must be closed to send the
                    current that way — one from the upper rail and one from the lower rail?
                    Write down the pair before reading on.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-diagonal-brake">
                    <statement><p>Now suppose you instead close both lower switches at
                    once, tying both motor terminals to ground while the motor is still
                    spinning.  Given that a spinning motor generates its own voltage, what
                    do you expect to happen?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                To drive current out of OUT1 and into OUT2 we close the upper switch on the
                OUT1 side and the lower switch on the OUT2 side — one diagonal of the H.
                The opposite diagonal reverses it.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-cw-ccw">
                <caption>The two driving diagonals.  On the left, the upper-left and
                lower-right switches are closed, so current flows from the supply through
                OUT1, through the motor, and back through OUT2 to ground — one direction of
                rotation.  On the right, the other diagonal is closed, and the current, and
                the rotation, are reversed.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide11_665f7016.png" width="48%"/>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide11_d4050cd8.png" width="48%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-hbridge-brake">
                <caption>Brake mode.  Both lower switches are closed, tying the two motor
                terminals together at ground.  The spinning motor's own back-EMF now drives
                a current through that short, which opposes the rotation and brings the
                motor quickly to rest.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide12_8fcc0651.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <insight>
                <title>Shoot-through</title>
                <p>
                    One combination is dangerous: closing the upper and lower switch on the
                    <em>same</em> side at once connects the supply straight to ground
                    through the two switches, a near short called
                    <term>shoot-through</term> that can destroy them.  The switches take
                    real time to turn off, so even a clean-looking reversal can pass briefly
                    through this state.  The TB6612 contains logic that staggers its
                    switching so this cannot happen — one reason we drive a motor through a
                    driver IC and never wire the bridge by hand.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-tb6612">
            <title>Part 3: The TB6612 Driver and Its Truth Table</title>
            <p>
                The <term>TB6612</term> packages two complete H-bridges — the switches
                themselves and the logic that prevents shoot-through — onto one small
                <term>breakout board</term>, a circuit board with the chip already soldered
                down and its pins brought out to header pins we can wire by hand.  It
                exposes each bridge through a few logic-level pins, driven at the ordinary
                0 V and 3.3 V logic levels the STM32C031C6 puts out, so we never wire the
                four switches ourselves.  Each bridge can pass roughly 1.2 A to its motor;
                draw much more than that and the chip protects itself by shutting down until
                it cools.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-internal">
                <caption>Inside the TB6612 — the full chip, which holds two independent
                motor channels, A and B.  This course uses channel A: its three logic
                inputs, labeled AIN1, AIN2, and PWMA on the breakout (the IN1, IN2, and PWM
                of the text), pass through the control logic that drives the four switch
                gates, and the switches connect channel A's two outputs (AO1, AO2) to the
                motor supply VM or to ground.  The motor's large current flows only in the
                output half; the input side is ordinary 3.3 V logic.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide15_d0880cf7.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Two inputs, IN1 and IN2, choose what the bridge does, and a third, PWM,
                switches it on and off for speed control (Part 5).  A fourth pin, STBY
                (standby), must be HIGH for the driver to do anything at all; on our
                breakout it is pulled up already.  How the inputs map to the four modes — clockwise,
                counter-clockwise, brake, and stop — is not something to memorize or guess.
                It is printed in the datasheet.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-truth-table">
                <caption>The TB6612 <q>H-SW Control Function</q> table, from the datasheet
                (<c>external/datasheets/TB6612FNG_datasheet_en_20141001.pdf</c>, p. 4).
                IN1, IN2, and PWM set the outputs OUT1 and OUT2, and so the mode.
                <q>H/L</q> in a cell means the value in that position does not matter for
                that row.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide16_87126f38.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-truth-table">
                <title>Read the driver's truth table</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Open the datasheet to the <q>H-SW Control Function</q> table, shown
                    in <xref ref="fig-tb6612-truth-table"/>, and read off the input settings
                    for each mode.  Assume STBY is HIGH and PWM is HIGH throughout.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-tt-cw">
                    <statement><p>What values of IN1 and IN2 turn the motor clockwise (CW)?
                    Counter-clockwise (CCW)?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-tt-brake">
                    <statement><p>What values of IN1 and IN2 put the motor into short brake?
                    And what single change puts the whole driver into standby?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor>
                <p>From the table, with STBY and PWM both HIGH: clockwise is IN1 = HIGH,
                IN2 = LOW (OUT1 HIGH, OUT2 LOW); counter-clockwise is IN1 = LOW, IN2 = HIGH.
                Short brake is IN1 = HIGH, IN2 = HIGH, which drives both outputs LOW.
                Driving STBY LOW puts the whole driver into standby, with the outputs off
                (high impedance), whatever the other inputs are doing.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-direction">
            <title>Part 4: First Exercise — Direction by Hand</title>
            <p>
                Now we wire the driver and a motor and drive it directly, with no program
                yet, to watch the truth table come to life.  The motor needs its own current
                path and more than the 3.3 V logic can give, so it runs from a separate
                supply.  A 6–12 V wall adapter plugs into the barrel jack of a small
                regulator board; on that board an L7805 regulator brings the input down to a
                steady 5 V and brings it out on a header, and we feed that 5 V to the
                driver's VM pin.  The driver's logic side, VCC, runs from the Nucleo's
                3.3 V.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-regulator">
                <caption>The regulator board that supplies the motor.  A 6–12 V wall adapter
                plugs into the barrel jack (J1); the L7805 regulator (U1), with its two
                smoothing capacitors (C1 and C2, 0.22 µF and 0.1 µF), brings that down to a
                steady 5 V; and each of the two headers (J2 and J4) brings out three pins —
                5V, GND, and Vin.  Take the motor supply from the <em>5V</em> pin.  The
                <em>Vin</em> pin is the raw, unregulated 6–12 V input: wiring it to VM would
                drive the motor at far more than its rated 3–6 V.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide17_img2.png" width="32%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The 5 V motor supply and the 3.3 V logic supply share a common ground — one
                wire joins the regulator board's GND to the Nucleo's ground — but are
                otherwise separate.  That separation is worth getting right: the motor and
                VM sit on the 5 V rail, so as long as no 5 V wire lands on the 3.3 V rail, a
                mistake on the motor side stays on the motor side and does not reach the
                3.3 V parts of the board.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-wiring">
                <caption>The breakout and motor for the first exercise.  The TB6612 breakout
                takes the 5 V motor supply at VM (from the regulator board above), the
                Nucleo's 3.3 V at VCC, and the shared ground; the motor connects to the two
                channel-A outputs.  For this exercise the PWM pin is held HIGH and the two
                direction wires, AIN1 and AIN2, are left free, so you can move them by hand
                between the power and ground rails.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide17_1573fa6d.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <!-- FIGURE GAP flagged to Petra at Gate 2: the Fritzing (slide17_1573fa6d)
                 routes VM from the Nucleo 3V3 pin, not from the regulator's 5 V rail.
                 Needs Petra's corrected wiring figure (VM fed from the regulated 5 V), and
                 confirmation that AIN1/AIN2 are shown free rather than already wired. -->
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-direction">
                <title>Drive the motor's direction by hand</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Build the circuit with the power off first, then power it and move the
                    direction wires by hand.  Keep the truth table
                    (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-truth-table"/>), shown again here, in front of
                    you.</p>
                    <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide16_87126f38.png" width="70%"/>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-wire">
                    <statement><p>With everything unpowered, wire it up: the breakout's VCC
                    to the Nucleo's 3.3 V; the breakout's VM to the regulator board's 5V pin;
                    and a common ground joining the Nucleo's ground, the breakout's GND, and
                    the regulator board's GND.  Connect the motor to the two channel-A
                    outputs.  Tie the PWM pin HIGH, to the 3.3 V rail, and leave the AIN1 and
                    AIN2 wires free.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-predict">
                    <statement><p>You will move AIN1 and AIN2 between the 3.3 V rail (call it
                    <q>+</q>) and ground (<q>&#8722;</q>).  Using the truth table, predict the
                    motor's behavior for each of the four settings of (AIN1, AIN2): (+, +),
                    (+, &#8722;), (&#8722;, +), and (&#8722;, &#8722;).  Write your four
                    predictions down before applying power.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-verify">
                    <statement><p>Power the circuit — plug the wall adapter into the regulator
                    board for the motor's 5 V, and connect the Nucleo to your computer over
                    USB for the 3.3 V logic — then move the AIN1 and AIN2 wires through all
                    four settings.  Does each match your prediction?  If the motor does
                    nothing in any of the four settings, check that the regulator's 5 V
                    actually reaches VM before checking the individual wires; if a single
                    setting is wrong, check that one wire against the truth table.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-dir-stretch">
                    <statement><p>If you finish early: from the truth table, predict what the
                    motor does if you also move the PWM wire from the 3.3 V rail down to
                    ground, then try it.  This is a first look at how PWM will control the
                    motor in Part 5.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor>
                <p>With PWM held HIGH: (+, +), both inputs HIGH, is short brake;
                (+, &#8722;) is IN1 HIGH, IN2 LOW, clockwise; (&#8722;, +) is
                counter-clockwise; and (&#8722;, &#8722;), both LOW, is stop, with the
                outputs off so the motor coasts.  The two settings where the inputs differ
                are the two turning diagonals from Part 2; the two settings where they match
                are the brake and the coast.  The stretch: with PWM tied LOW, both outputs go
                LOW whatever the direction inputs say — short brake — so the motor stops
                turning.  That is the extreme, a 0% duty cycle, of the speed control we build
                in Part 5.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-pwm">
            <title>Part 5: PWM Sets the Speed</title>
            <p>
                The H-bridge sets the motor's direction.  In this part we turn to its
                speed, which we set with PWM.  The reading showed the difficulty: a GPIO pin
                is either fully HIGH or fully LOW, with nothing in between, so it cannot hold
                an in-between voltage.  Nor does the STM32C031C6 have a true DAC to do it for
                us — the DAC we met on Day 7 lived inside the ADC, making test voltages the
                program never sees, not a pin we can drive to an analog level.  So rather
                than hold the motor at some steady in-between voltage, we switch the full
                supply on and off quickly and let the <term>duty cycle</term> — the fraction
                of each period spent HIGH — set the average.  The motor's inertia smooths the
                pulses, and it turns as though driven by that average voltage.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-waveform">
                <caption>PWM at three duty cycles, drawn for a 5 V supply.  The output is a
                fixed-frequency square wave; the wider the HIGH pulse, the higher the
                average voltage (dashed line) and the faster the motor.  Here a 75% duty
                cycle averages 3.75 V, a 50% duty cycle averages 2.5 V, and a 20% duty
                cycle averages 1 V.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide23_b58b0b83.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The waveform is produced with almost no work.  A counter counts up from
                zero and resets when it reaches a fixed top value, over and over.  A second
                number, the compare value, is checked against the counter: while the counter
                is below the compare value the output is HIGH, and once it reaches the
                compare value the output goes LOW until the counter resets.  A larger compare
                value keeps the output HIGH for more of each period — a larger duty cycle.
                The whole thing runs in the timer hardware, so once it is set up the CPU does
                nothing to keep it going.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-counter-compare">
                <caption>How the timer makes the waveform.  A counter counts up and resets
                at its top value, over and over (the blue ramp).  A compare value (the
                dashed line) is checked against it: while the counter is below the compare
                value the output is HIGH, and above it the output is LOW.  Raising the
                compare value keeps the ramp below the line for more of each period, which
                widens the HIGH pulse and so raises the duty cycle.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/pwm-counter-compare.svg" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                We will use timer TIM14 for this — the same timer we set up for periodic
                interrupts on Day 8 — and route its output onto a pin.  Not every pin can
                carry a given timer's output; the datasheet's pin table lists which can.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-pwm">
                <title>Find the pin, and predict the speeds</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>We will drive the PWM from pin PA7 (Arduino header D11).  Two quick
                    tasks set up the exercise that follows.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-pwm-pin">
                    <statement><p>In the datasheet's Table 12, <em>Pin assignment and
                    description</em> (p. 30), find PA7 and read off which timer channel it
                    can output as one of its alternate functions.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-pwm-predict">
                    <statement><p>The program you are about to run ramps the duty cycle up
                    over time.  Before running it, write down how you expect the motor's
                    speed to compare at roughly 25%, 50%, and 75% duty cycle.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor>
                <p>PA7's alternate functions include TIM14_CH1, its alternate function 4 —
                that is the channel we drive.  As for the speeds: the average voltage is
                proportional to the duty cycle, and the speed roughly follows the average
                voltage, so the motor should turn about three times as fast at 75% as at
                25%, with 50% in between — allowing that at very low duty cycles friction may
                keep it from turning at all until the average voltage is large enough to
                start it.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day11-demo">
            <title>Part 6: Second Exercise — Ramping the Motor with PWM</title>
            <p>
                For the last exercise we hand the driver's inputs over to the Nucleo and run
                a program that ramps the motor's speed up and down and reverses it.  The
                wiring continues from the first exercise: leave the power, the motor, and the
                breakout as they are, and move three wires.  The PWM pin, which you tied HIGH
                by hand, now goes to PA7 (D11); the AIN1 and AIN2 wires, which you moved by
                hand, now go to PA5 (D13) and PA6 (D12), so the program controls all three.
                If your first-exercise circuit is not working, you can instead wire the
                second-exercise circuit (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-wiring-2"/>) from scratch.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tb6612-wiring-2">
                <caption>Wiring for the second exercise, changed from the first only at the
                driver's three logic inputs: PWM to PA7 (D11), AIN1 to PA5 (D13), AIN2 to
                PA6 (D12).  The motor and its 5 V supply are unchanged.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide27_faf84a1a.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Make a copy of <c>TemplateProject</c>, rename it <c>DCmotor</c>, and add the
                demonstration program <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> to its <c>Src</c> folder.
                Compile and run it.  The program sets up TIM14 for PWM on PA7, drives AIN1
                and AIN2 for direction, and repeatedly ramps the duty cycle up and back down,
                reversing the motor at the ends of the ramp.  You will run it today; we will
                go through how it programs TIM14 in the next class.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-demo">
                <title>Run the ramp and watch the PWM</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>With the program running and the motor turning, put the oscilloscope
                    on the PWM signal so you can watch the duty cycle change as the motor
                    speeds up and slows down.  If the motor does not move when the program
                    runs, confirm the build succeeded and that the three wires reached PA5,
                    PA6, and PA7.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-demo-scope">
                    <statement><p>Connect oscilloscope channel 1 (the orange lead) to PA7 and
                    its ground lead to a ground on the board.  As the motor ramps, which way
                    does the HIGH portion of each period change as the motor speeds
                    up?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-demo-check">
                    <statement><p>Compare what you see with the speeds you predicted in
                    Part 5.  Does a wider HIGH pulse go with a faster motor, as a higher
                    average voltage should?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor>
                <p>As the motor speeds up, the HIGH portion of each PWM period grows wider: a
                larger duty cycle, a larger average voltage, and a faster motor, exactly as
                predicted.  The period itself stays fixed — only the width of the HIGH part
                changes.</p>
            </instructor>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwm-scope">
                <caption>The PWM signal from <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> on the oscilloscope as the
                motor ramps.  The period is fixed; the width of the HIGH portion — the duty
                cycle — grows and shrinks as the program raises and lowers the motor
                speed.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day11-Motors/slide29_ab2c785e.jpg" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day11-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Two questions that go past simply running the demo.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-stretch-freq">
                    <statement><p>The timer feeds its counter a 2 MHz clock — the 12 MHz
                    system clock divided by 6 — and the counter counts to 1250 before it
                    resets.  Work out the PWM frequency from those two numbers, then measure
                    the period of the waveform on the oscilloscope and check that it
                    agrees.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day11-stretch-double">
                    <statement><p>The motor's speed roughly follows the duty cycle, and the
                    duty cycle is the compare value out of 1250.  If the motor is running at a
                    compare value of 300 and you want to roughly double its speed, what
                    compare value would you try?  Why only <q>roughly</q>?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor>
                <p>Frequency is the counter clock divided by the count it resets at:
                12,000,000 Hz ÷ 6 = 2,000,000 Hz feeds the counter, and 2,000,000 Hz ÷ 1250 =
                1,600 Hz = 1.6 kHz — a period of about 625 µs, which is what the scope shows.
                For the second question: doubling the duty cycle means roughly compare
                value 600, since speed follows the average voltage and the average voltage
                follows the duty cycle.  It is only <q>roughly</q> because that relationship
                is not perfectly linear — friction and back-EMF bend it, and at a low enough
                duty cycle the motor may not turn at all.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                For next class, work through <c>TTmotor_ramp.c</c> with the reference manual
                open — RM0490, <q>General-purpose timers (TIM14)</q>, section 17.3.8
                (<em>PWM mode</em>) and section 17.4 (<em>TIM14 registers</em>) — and see how
                the program sets the timer's registers to produce the PWM you watched today.
                In the next class we will build that setup ourselves.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- Day 11x and Day 12 in-class sections, and the chapter's end-of-chapter
         Reference material, are authored in separate sessions.  The Day 11x material
         covers writing the TIM14 PWM code (CCMR1/CCER/CCR1) and begins motor speed
         sensing with the photointerrupter; Day 12 continues speed sensing and the
         full Lab 6 build.  Do not reconstruct the driver code from memory (B-6):
         recover it from the Day 11x deck's code slides, as recorded in
         plans/day11-ground-truth.md. -->


</chapter>
